--TEST--
`exit("…")` writes its message and ends with status zero, PHP's other spelling of the one construct — docs/adr/README.md § Decisions taken at project start
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:programs/ending-a-program
// The operand carries both of PHP's spellings at once: an `int` is the
// process status, and a `string` is a message written first, after which the
// status is `0`. Anything else is a type mismatch at the operand, since
// `rule:types/conversion` has no implicit conversion to offer there.
string $msg = "goodbye\n";
echo "start\n";
exit($msg);
--EXPECT--
start
goodbye
